{
  "_comment": "PHASE 9.5B Stage 4.1 baseline. Each entry is a property whose catalogue default differs from the fallback literal in its executor. This is latent, not active: the catalogue default is written into every node on creation, so the executor fallback only applies to a legacy asset that omits the property entirely -- there the runtime and the editor disagree. Reconciling all 66 would change authoring defaults across the palette, which is a gameplay change and out of scope for Stage 4.1. Recorded so the test fails on NEW divergence. PHASE 9 recovery item 4.2: play_animation.animation_name and play_animation.state left this baseline because the mismatch is gone, not because the check was relaxed. The node was declared twice; the losing declaration defaulted animation_name to 'idle' while the executor read that same key, and the winning declaration had no 'state' pin at all. animation_nodes now owns the node, 'state' is the declared authoring property with an empty default, and the executor reads it -- so declaration and executor agree and there is nothing left to record. PHASE 9 recovery item 8: set_variable.name and set_variable.value ENTERED this baseline, and not because anything regressed. The node had two executors; the misc_nodes one defaulted name to 'value' and so happened to match the declaration, masking the fallbacks of the scene_nodes executor that actually wins at runtime. Item 8 removed the duplicate, and the real executor's fallbacks became visible: name '' and value 0. Reconciling them is the same gameplay-adjacent authoring-default change this baseline exists to defer -- writing a variable literally called 'value', or writing '' where the executor writes 0, are behaviour decisions. Latent as ever: the catalogue seeds both properties on creation, so the fallbacks only apply to a graph that omits them entirely. PHASE 9 recovery item 9: move.speed LEFT this baseline because the mismatch is gone, not because the check was relaxed. It recorded declared 100.0 against executor fallback 200.0. The fallback was unreachable -- the catalogue seeds speed into every normalized node, so it only applied to a graph carrying none, and all five shipping graphs author it explicitly (200/180/220/220/160). Measured displacement identical before and after aligning the fallback to the declared 100.",
